{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-72361",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Notice that if we try to substitute $x=1$ directly into the limit, we reach the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{f(x)+x}{g(x)-x} & ={\\frac{f(x)+x}{g(x)-x}|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(1)+1}{g(1)-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1+1}{1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we use L'Hopital's rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{f(x)+x}{g(x)-x} & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{(f(x)+x)^{′}}{(g(x)-x)^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)+1}{{g}^{′}(x)-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=1$ again, but once more, we reach the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)+1}{{g}^{′}(x)-1} & ={\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)+1}{{g}^{′}(x)-1}|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(1)+1}{{g}^{′}(1)-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1+1}{1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe apply L'Hopital's rule again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)+1}{{g}^{′}(x)-1} & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{({f}^{′}(x)+1)^{′}}{({g}^{′}(x)-1)^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{f}^{″}(x)}{{g}^{″}(x)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand this time, when we substitute $x=1,$ we are actually able to determine the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{f}^{″}(x)}{{g}^{″}(x)} & ={\\frac{{f}^{″}(x)}{{g}^{″}(x)}|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{f}^{″}(1)}{{g}^{″}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{-2} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{f(x)+x}{g(x)-x}=-2.$",
